thinking_content: "**Continuing research activity**\n\nI need to push forward with my research activities, particularly aiming for the state-of-the-art (SOTA) score, which is currently 0.7452. I have several results to consider, like our DAQB at 0.7365 and others below that. The rules say I can run only two evaluations concurrently, which I’m already doing. While waiting for results, I could either read, plan, or reflect in the Reflection Chamber. Yet, I can't access the Archive Room until I reach maturity.**Preparing for publication**\n\nI can’t publish just yet, but I can work on a draft in private memory. This area is available, though it uses storage when I write. I could add updates to the research_log or review the lineages of other anomalies. I should also check the evaluation logs for evaluations 926 and 927, as they might provide more detailed metrics, including any raw values or debug notes. However, it seems there are no insights into the specific \"uns\" data from the logs.**Analyzing\
  \ power schedule results**\n\nI could print the lambda values, but those prints would go to stdout since we haven't done that yet. For selection of the power schedule, we didn’t print lambda, so maybe I should modify the code to include it and the distributions of R2 and gamma. While printing will increase the logs, it's acceptable. We have a concurrency limit of two, so I can make these changes and prepare for submission later. \n\nAnalyzing the power schedule, it's clear we have high Graph_conn and cLISI scores, but the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) shows a decrease relative to the reanchor. Our modifications are close but not above the reanchor score, which indicates we’re on the right track, but still have work to do to reach our target.**Identifying improvement factors**\n\nI think the difference in performance could come from the GraphFWPCA parameters, particularly alpha, kd, and auto-lambda settings. The 846 score was achieved using Auto-λ v2 with a target_s of 0.04 and kd at 20. Our\
  \ 'reanchor' pipeline refers to the DAQB Recommended approach, which seems to utilize the auto-lambda v2 mean-target but not GraphFWPCA weighting on the graph path. To improve our scores, we likely need to incorporate the GraphFWPCA weighting with alpha_g around 0.5, along with the MPB protocol.**Planning next steps for improvement**\n\nI could adjust δ within a range of 0.09 to 0.11 and attempt to replicate or surpass the score of 0.7445. Previous attempts show that these adjustments had mixed results, and I need to consider the impact of changing lambda. The top score utilized lambda adjustments, but it’s crucial to ensure improvements align with the intended protocols. I might also explore the Graph Weighted PCA approach, though I should be cautious about introducing new methods that could affect reproducibility. \n\nSo, while I wait for my current runs to finish, I’ll focus on outlining my next experiments and patching up my research logs. The experimentation will include analyzing\
  \ hyperparameters and incorporating findings from past evaluations to bridge the gap toward achieving that state-of-the-art performance. I think I’ll also take some time to reflect on these plans in the Reflection Chamber to ensure I’m considering all angles before proceeding.**Planning submission adjustments**\n\nI remember that we have the 'praxis_core' with weighted variables for embedding, and it's crucial as we consider implementing the 'MPB Graph FWPCA' with a whitening step before residualization. In my immediate code, the 'mpb_graphfwpca_override' allows a lambda override but I should wait until existing experiments finish before submitting new ones. \n\nI plan to write a new script for testing parameter configurations while preparing to submit two experiments soon, focusing on replicating results near SOTA. The aim is to tweak existing parameters carefully while ensuring we don't exceed concurrency limits. Submitting runs on adjusted lambda values could give us better insight\
  \ into our data's performance.**Finalizing submission actions**\n\nI can set delta (δ) to 0.09 or 0.11, but since scoreboard 869 shows 0.7398 for δ 0.09 and 870 at 0.7404 for δ 0.11, I'll choose δ 0.10. I’ll keep alpha_g at its default of 0.5. \n\nI need to ensure that sys.path includes 'storage' for importing resources, and I’ll replicate that in my submission. \n\nThe next steps are to submit two runs: one with lambda (λ) at 0.605 and δ 0.10, and another with λ at 0.650 and δ 0.10. I'll also write a log summary for documentation."
