    \   )\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.10 (kd=22, k=48) — kdensity probe\"\ntags: \"MPB, GraphFWPCA, DAQB, override, residualization, graph\"\nabstract: \"Test kdensity=22 at the current best λ=0.605, δ=0.10, k=48 to see if a slightly larger density neighborhood improves mixing/graph stability.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"[override] lam=0.605, delta=0.10, kd=22, k=48\")\n      return eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca(\n          adata,\n          lam_override=0.605,\n          delta=0.10,\n          kdensity=22,\n          k_total=48\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Testing parameters for optimization**\n\nI'm analyzing the results where λ 0.600 indicates a big drop, while λ 0.605 shows a slight drop. It seems the optimum δ might be 0.10 with λ around 0.605. I plan to test configurations with λ 0.610 and δ 0.10, looking into kdensity values at 18 and 22 as well. There was a better result earlier with kd 20, but I’m curious about kd 22. I might need to implement some changes regarding n_pcs_graph parameters, which means writing new submission code.**Planning tests for optimization**\n\nI can only run two tests concurrently, so I’ll choose λ 0.610 with δ 0.10 and kdensity 22 alongside λ 0.605 and δ 0.10. From earlier results, kd 20 performed best with a score of 846, while kd 18 had a score of 0.7375. There's a need to test kd 22 too. Alternatively, I could try λ 0.605 with δ 0.095 since δ 0.11 is showing promise as well. I’ll propose two tests to see if there's any improvement with these configurations.**Implementing logging for\
  \ parameters**\n\nI’m considering adding log prints to include parameters in STDOUT, but our current override function doesn’t support printing. The code for “mpb_graphfwpca_override” doesn’t print either, so I could create a small wrapper to print the λ and δ values. I can incorporate this into the submission code to display these arguments within the “eliminate_batch_effect_fn” wrapper. I’ll need to print before the call to return because printing afterward isn’t possible. I’ll also confirm that “k_total” should remain at 48, as that's standard.**Planning assistant message and actions**\n\nI think we should create a log addendum for our 'paper plan,' although it's not strictly necessary. I'm also considering exploring the Reflection Chamber, but that’s optional too. It’s good to note our token budget, which shows we have plenty left. Now, let's craft the final assistant message. I’ll provide insights about the results, mentioning that λ 0.600 shows a strong drop, while the optimum is\
  \ near 0.605. I’ll also outline our next plan with two submission actions using YAML blocks and ensure we follow the station's instructions for sequential execution.**Finalizing the assistant message**\n\nI’m ready to put together my final message. First, I’ll provide a brief reflection summary. Then, I’ll create two submission actions using the '/execute_action{submit 1}' command with YAML content. \n\nFor λ 0.610 and δ 0.10, the title will be: \"MPB-GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.610, δ=0.10 (kd=20, k=48) — λ flank.\" I’ll include relevant tags and the code necessary for execution. The second submission will test kd 22 with the title: \"MPB-GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.10 (kd=22, k=48) — kdensity test.\" I’m also noting performance data, where earlier scores suggest kd 22 might degrade, but I'll proceed with testing anyway. Adjusting page size isn’t necessary for this stage."
