  \ what \"MPB\" means in the context of \"MPB-GraphFWPCA.\"**Gathering information on metrics and experiments**\n\nI need to recall earlier research conducted by Praxis II, but I don't have the content of Archive #28 right now. I should read it to replicate the definitions. We can visit the Archive to access it later. Meanwhile, I want to gather the exact metrics for EIDs 960, 609, and possibly 938 baseline, which I have from the table. To be thorough, I could do a /review to get full logs, including any raw and normalized metrics. The evaluator likely needs the complete 10-metric vector for the generalization experiment, so I should prepare those.**Understanding generalization requirements**\n\nI'm considering how generalization can show robustness across seeds, which might satisfy the reviewer's request for a mandatory generalization experiment. They want us to evaluate under conditions not seen during training. Since we're using an unsupervised method, this could mean looking at alternative\
  \ embedding paths or shifts in dataset distribution. I can refer to earlier evaluations on Zcorr without GraphFWPCA, even if they scored around 0.61. I also need to showcase stability on the heart dataset across different k neighbors, but I'm uncertain if that will be sufficient.**Planning the generalization experiments**\n\nI’m considering checking the \"Submitted Evaluations\" to see if we've previously done a generalization test on Zcorr using PCA50 and other methods. While we can’t claim those results as our own, we can reference them. It seems necessary to run a new experiment for generalization, as we haven't submitted anything in a while. I plan to conduct generalization tests on the same dataset with different embedding paths and parameters, ensuring we meet the reviewer’s requirements for reproducibility. The implementation of the codes may become complex, but I can streamline our approach.**Executing the generalization test**\n\nI think we can run a generalization test using\
  \ our data to validate the robustness of our hyperparameter settings. While we have an existing evaluation with seed=1 yielding 0.738, the reviewer is looking for broader dataset-level generalization. We should include both replication by other lineages and seed variations in our submission. To expedite action, I can create a simplified submission with plain PCA, utilizing existing code. However, I also want to ensure we outline a complete methods section along with necessary metrics to satisfy the reviewer. It’s essential to balance time and thoroughness here.**Refining weight calculations and results**\n\nI’m deriving the weight \\( w_g = 1 / (1 + α r_g) \\) for decreasing batch-associated gene influence. Next, I'll build a neighbor graph through weighted PCA and apply the Balanced Residualized Batch Graph (BRBG) method. Afterward, I’ll also compute density-adaptive quotas. This involves calculating neighbor density to adjust target weights based on local density. \n\nAs for the results,\
  \ I’ll make sure to arrange them correctly according to specified metrics for clarity in submission. It’s crucial to package everything with full methods and relevant metrics tables for the Archive!**Preparing archive submission and code**\n\nI plan to include equations or pseudocode for both BRBG and DAQB in the archive. I’ll ensure we follow YAML formatting and keep the abstract concise. To address research inactivity warnings, we should submit a new evaluation as part of the generalization. I think it’s vital to prepare the archive correctly while also finding existing code to help create a new generalization run.\n\nI’ll begin by listing resources in storage to locate relevant scripts for our submission while considering the necessary EIDs and metrics that need to be included. Let's get started!"
