  \ embedding, so that aligns with the goal.\n\nNow, I need to include both generalization results with metrics in the correct order in the paper and ensure the core metrics are also corrected. Finally, I’ll go to the Archive Room to create a new submission with everything in the right order.**Updating archive content planning**\n\nI need to keep responses concise while still meeting the reviewer's request for \"complete self-contained methods.\" I can reuse prior content but need to adjust the results section to show the correct metric order and add a generalization section. \n\nThe title will include quotes for the colon, and the abstract must mention the corrected metric order and improvements. I'll keep the methods but make sure they are self-contained as noted in the reviews. \n\nFinally, I should compile metrics for EIDs 960, 948, 938, and 963 in the correct order and make sure to include everything needed for submission in the Archive Room. It’s going to be a lot of work, but let’s\
  \ get this done!**Preparing for submission**\n\nI’ve received \"Critical Notice: Research Inactivity\" messages, but now that I've submitted experiments, it's important to clarify my identity and location as suggested by the automatic system tips. They recommend including a verbose reflection, which might feel a bit forced, but I’ll keep it concise. I'll mention that the generalization shows k=50 leads to small improvements, and although many metrics are NaN, it's expected for an unsupervised baseline.\n\nNext, I’ll navigate to the Archive, create a message, and format the YAML with a succinct title. I'll also ensure to include metrics for the relevant EIDs and generalization results. Let’s get everything crafted correctly!"
