thinking_content: "**Reevaluating auto-tuning metrics**\n\nThe label-free auto-tuning attempts show low iLISI due to unchanged neighbors. The J measure on symmetric top-k didn't alter neighbor identity, meaning it’s more about selecting from candidate k sets. Evaluating Jaccard similarity and aiming for minimal J to enhance connectivity could lead to poor mixing if k is too small or large. The iLISI 0.007 indicates over-separation. We should develop a better auto-selection criterion that aligns with final integrated metrics without using labels, possibly through the Graph Health Score.**Assessing batch mixing metrics**\n\nI can compute \"batch mixing entropy\" by using batch labels since integration involves both batch labels and gene expression. The specification allows this, so it's fine. While evaluation metrics also assess batch mixing and generally desire label mixing, we could look into measuring adjacency mixing. Our earlier attempts with the \"J\" metric for determining Jaccard change\
  \ didn’t improve mixing, so I need a new approach, like measuring \"quota imbalance vs. distance\" or \"Graph connectivity parity.\" Using local connectivity might ensure that the kNN graph remains connected without relying on cell type labels.**Exploring connectivity metrics**\n\nThe Graph_conn metric relies on cell type labels to measure connectivity within the same type, but that's not applicable here. Instead, I can compute a general connectivity fraction, focusing on the largest connected component and modularity using just batch labels, though that may lead to oversmoothing. A better method could be the \"Local Cross-Batch Ratio\" (LCBR), which measures cross-batch neighbors against k. However, this might ignore biological nuances if not balanced. Another option is to calculate \"quota pressure residual\" to see how many edges adjusted to meet specified quotas, guiding k selection to maximize iLISI with batch labels.**Calculating label-free metrics**\n\nI can compute an approximate\
  \ \"batch LISI\" using batch labels, which is permissible. My selection will aim for kd and k values that yield iLISI above the 10th percentile of the baseline, while also ensuring \"local preservation\" by correlating distances before and after residualization. Although the specification prohibits using cell_type, batch labels are fine. I can implement this in code to compute iLISI and adjust hyperparameters adaptively. The official evaluation uses exact iLISI metrics, but creating a 'fast' iLISI to guide selection is allowed.**Setting up function and storage reads**\n\nFirst, I need to create a function that computes the graph based on variable k and kd, returning both connectivities and distances. Then, I’ll set up auto-selection loops for candidate sets, but since we have an evaluation limit of 30 minutes, I’ll restrict it to 6 combinations. Including 'no_debugger: true' as a default sounds reasonable. \n\nNext, I’m ready to execute the 'storage read' actions since it’s allowed in\
  \ the Research Counter. For now, I'll prioritize these files: 'praxis/best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50.py', 'praxis/mpb_graphfwpca_override.py', 'praxis/brbg_common.py', 'praxis/bbsg_density_adaptive.py', and 'shared/diagnostics/graph_health_v3.py'. That should be sufficient to get started!"
