parts:
- text: "Masked-weights attempt 1 (1019) was auto-debugged into a default MPB run (lam=0.70), so it’s not the intended test. True masked-weights row-normalized variant (1020) is running with lam=0.605. If <0.742, I’ll end the weighting line and publish a short negative‑results addendum.\n\n`/execute_action{rank author}`\n\nPreparing an addendum draft in Private Memory for swift Archive submission after cooldown.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft: Weighted-connectivity on DAQB mask over MPB‑GraphFWPCA — negative result plan\"\ncontent: |\n  Scope\n  - Goal: Test whether restoring informative edge weights on the SOTA DAQB edge set improves over binary connectivities.\n  - Base: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG (λ=0.605) + DAQB (δ=0.10, kd=22), k=50, cap=2.0, seed=0.\n\n  Experiments\n  - Auto‑k via Graph Health (ID 1011): 0.7396. Selected (k=50,kd=22). Full metrics (spec order): [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.713, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000,\
    \ 0.327].\n  - Auto‑kd via Graph Health (ID 1013): 0.7373. Selected kd=24@k=50. Metrics: [0.659, 0.248, 0.758, 0.714, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.305].\n  - Masked weights v1 (ID 1019): 0.7309. NOTE: auto‑debugger rerouted to default MPB run (lam=0.70), so not the intended masked‑weights test.\n  - Masked weights v2 (row‑norm) (ID 1020): running. True masked‑weights implementation using DAQB mask + EtC neighbor weights with row normalization and symmetrization.\n\n  SOTA Reference\n  - Fixed SOTA (ID 960): 0.7465 at (k=50,kd=22, λ=0.605, δ=0.10, cap=2.0, seed=0). Metrics: [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366].\n\n  Interim Conclusion\n  - Graph‑health auto‑selection underperforms fixed SOTA near the ridge.\n  - Masked‑weights v1 was invalid (debug reroute). v2 will decide; expectation: ≤SOTA due to label‑metric sensitivity.\n\n  Plan\n  - If 1020 < 0.742, close weighting line and publish a concise negative‑results addendum with full vectors (1011,\
    \ 1013, 1019 note, 1020).\n  - Future: pursue multi‑objective label‑free selector (graph health + stability proxies) rather than weights.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Preparing for next steps**


  While waiting, I think it''s a good idea to read the log from the 1019 result to confirm the lambda value, which was set to the default of 0.70. We can''t change the current setup now, but I can note that the first submission shouldn''t be marked as a mask. I’ll remain focused on research hygiene and prepare my next steps, including creating a new private memory capsule about weights on the DAQB mask and adding key details there.**Planning for metrics and actions**


  Right now, I can’t know the metrics for 1020, so I’m thinking about using placeholders in the meantime. It might be helpful to record our expectations—maybe something like, "Monitoring 1020; if the score is less than 0.742, we''ll close the weighting line." I’ll avoid new submissions since we have one running. I might also propose unifying Graph Health with multi-objective structures. Overall, I need to keep my final message brief and ensure I follow the station''s parsing rules.**Summarizing debug effects and plans**


  I think I should provide a brief summary of the 1019 debug effects and outline expectations for 1020, including plans to stop the weighting line. Once the archive cooldown is lifted, I''ll write an addendum. I might consider ranking the author to show our runs and I could reflect in the Reflection Chamber, but that’s optional. I plan to create a capsule called "Addendum draft: Weighted masked edges" in private memory for future use when the archive is ready.**Creating addendum for future reference**


  I’m planning to create a new addendum titled "Addendum: Weighted edges on DAQB mask: negative result." It will include a concise bulleted summary covering 1011, 1013, 1019, and the pending 1020 results to keep the tokens light. My next steps will involve executing the action to go to private memory, then creating a YAML with the title and summary for later publishing. I just need to remember to quote the colons correctly in the YAML. Let’s move forward!'
