parts:
- text: "Submitting the missing generalization experiment (binary vs weighted) and drafting the formal paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization: Vanilla kNN on MPB‑GraphFWPCA (k=50, binary connectivities)\"\ntags: \"baseline, generalization, knn, binary, MPB-GraphFWPCA\"\nabstract: \"We build a vanilla kNN graph (k=50) on the MPB‑GraphFWPCA X_emb and set connectivities to binary 1.0 on existing edges (distances preserved). This tests whether edge weights help on a simpler baseline by comparing to the weighted vanilla kNN result (EID 994 = 0.3039).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, numpy as np, scipy.sparse as sp\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  import mpb_graphfwpca_override as mpb\n\n  def _ensure_csr(m):\n      return m.tocsr() if hasattr(m, \"tocsr\") else sp.csr_matrix(m)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Spec normalization from\
    \ raw counts\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # Get MPB‑GraphFWPCA embedding (X_emb). We will override the graph.\n      adata = mpb.eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca(\n          adata,\n          rng_seed=0\n      )\n\n      # Vanilla kNN on X_emb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, n_neighbors=50, use_rep='X_emb', metric='euclidean', random_state=0)\n      C = _ensure_csr(adata.obsp['connectivities']).astype(np.float32)\n      D = _ensure_csr(adata.obsp['distances']).astype(np.float32)\n\n      # Binary connectivities on the same edge set; symmetrize by max\n      C.data[:] = 1.0\n      Cb = C.maximum(C.T)\n      Db = D.maximum(D.T)\n\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = _ensure_csr(Cb)\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = _ensure_csr(Db)\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft:\
    \ Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)\"\ncontent: |\n  Title\n  Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration on Human Heart, with baseline generalization.\n\n  Abstract\n  We study whether restoring edge weights on quota-balanced graphs (DAQB) improves integration. On the MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB pipeline (Human Heart), reinstating weights on the DAQB-selected edges degrades the primary score and mixing (iLISI) relative to binary connectivities with true distances. Auto-selection of k/kd via label-free graph-health proxies also underperforms the fixed SOTA ridge. We include a baseline generalization on a simpler vanilla kNN graph to test whether edge weights help there. Across ≥5 experiments, binary connectivities remain preferred under this spec.\n\n  Introduction\n  Batch integration balances removal of technical effects with conservation of biological signal. Graph construction choices (binary\
    \ vs weighted connectivities) are widely assumed to help. We evaluate this assumption within an Embed‑then‑Correct family using MPB‑GraphFWPCA, BRBG (λ-shrinkage), and DAQB, and we test a simpler vanilla kNN baseline.\n\n  Methods\n  - Data: Human Heart (~20k × 2k HVGs), counts→normalize_total(1e4)→log1p. No labels used.\n  - Embedding: MPB‑GraphFWPCA (Praxis lineage), PCA dims=100 for graph path; EtC X_emb for QC neighbors.\n  - BRBG: column-balanced quotas with λ=0.605, cap=2.0; shrinkage toward uniform.\n  - DAQB: density-adaptive quotas with kd=22, δ=0.10; final k=50 neighbors; symmetric CSR connectivities and distances.\n  - Weighted vs binary: “Binary” sets connectivities=1.0 on selected edges; distances are true Euclidean neighbor distances. “Masked-weights” reinstates vanilla scanpy weights onto DAQB-selected edges, with/without row-normalization.\n\n  Experiments (spec-ordered metric vectors)\n  - SOTA reference (ID 960, k=50,kd=22, λ=0.605, δ=0.10): score 0.7465 → [ASW_batch\
    \ 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.774, NMI 0.728, Graph_conn 0.968, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.961, cLISI 0.973, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.366].\n  - Auto‑k (ID 1011): 0.7396 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.713, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327].\n  - Auto‑kd (ID 1013): 0.7373 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.758, 0.714, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.305].\n  - Masked‑weights v1 (ID 1019, note: auto‑debug reroute): 0.7309 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.674, 0.693, 0.996, 0.791, 0.950, 0.971, 1.000, 0.327].\n  - Masked‑weights v2 row‑norm (ID 1020, true test): 0.6626 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.626, 0.722, 0.913, 0.789, 0.352, 0.990, 1.000, 0.327].\n  - Generalization (weighted vanilla kNN on X_emb, k=50) (ID 994): 0.3039 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.442, 0.658, 0.935, 0.000, 0.032, 0.971, 0.000, 0.000].\n  - Generalization (binary vanilla kNN on X_emb, k=50) (PENDING EID): to be inserted; method identical to 994 but connectivities binarized; distances preserved.\n\n  Results\n  - On DAQB-selected graphs, restoring weights sharply\
    \ degrades iLISI (to 0.3516 raw; normalized 0.352) and drops ARI/NMI, confirming binary connectivities with true distances are preferable at SOTA settings.\n  - Auto‑k/kd near the ridge cannot outperform fixed SOTA.\n  - Baseline generalization (vanilla kNN): will report binary vs weighted once PENDING EID completes, testing whether weights help on a simpler graph.\n\n  Discussion and Conclusion\n  Weighting DAQB-selected edges harms mixing and label preservation under this spec, suggesting quota-balanced topology, not weight magnitudes, carries the performance. Baseline results probe whether this insight transfers to simpler graphs. Future work: label‑free multi‑objective selectors and cross‑dataset checks.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning experiments for graph analysis**\n\nI think we need one more experiment along with a generalization experiment. They suggested we show that restoring edge weights might be harmful on a simpler baseline graph. I could run a baseline vanilla kNN graph with weighted versus binary configurations. We can compare the primary scores for the MPB-GraphFWPCA embedding. I feel we’ve already got the generalization results, but these didn’t include BRBG/DAQB, and I’m curious about that relationship with weights. Testing weighted versus binary in the vanilla kNN baseline might produce some interesting insights!**Examining evaluation for graph embeddings**\n\nI think we can quickly submit a new evaluation for the MPB-GraphFWPCA embedding. We would build top-k neighbors using scanpy while keeping connectivities and distances. This gives us the vanilla kNN baseline with weights, which has already shown a performance of 994/993. Now, I need to create a matched binary variant\
  \ by setting connectivities to 1.0 while keeping distances unchanged. The performance is low at 0.30 with binary settings, so I wonder if improving connectivities helps, even if it could go either way!**Designing experiments for weight comparison**\n\nIn the masked-weights experiments, it seems that weighting hurt performance compared to binary, so I think we should run both configurations on the baseline. I can create one pair with binary connectivities and another with weights to demonstrate that restoring weights also harms performance. We need a new submission for “Vanilla MPB-GraphFWPCA neighbors (k=50)” comparing binary versus weighted. Since we can only submit one evaluation at a time, I plan to run two sequential submissions. Let’s see if the binary variant for k=50 can outperform the weighted result!**Planning next steps for experiments**\n\nI'm thinking it's not guaranteed whether the weights will harm performance further, especially since we're seeing a score of 0.3039 with\
  \ the 994 configuration. The reviewer suggested running one more experiment, possibly by masking weights onto DAQB-selected edges using simple rescaling or column normalization. We could also add symmetric normalization or Jaccard re-normalization. To address the reviewer's feedback, I might need to propose revisions to the paper while queuing the baseline binary test. I'll work on both submitting the test and drafting the paper!**Implementing experiments and drafting paper**\n\nI need to implement a new research submission focused on generalization with \"Vanilla kNN on MPB-GraphFWPCA (k=50, binary connectivities).\" However, I want to avoid using BRBG+DAQB in computations. I can normalize counts and run the evaluation with minimal changes. Given the previous results, I suspect that binary may produce similar performance as weighted, potentially demonstrating their detrimental effects. \n\nI aim to submit a baseline binary evaluation and draft a formal paper outlining the methods, experimental\
  \ setup, and results in Private Memory while waiting for results!**Implementing baseline evaluation**\n\nI need to ensure proper concurrency limits when running my experiments. To start, I’ll create a baseline evaluation function to normalize the counts and compute PCA for 100 dimensions, using binary connectivities. This avoids complexities with the MPB embedding, which reduces risk.\n\nI plan to run two experiments: one for weighted connectivities and another with binary. This will answer the reviewer's request for demonstrating that restoring weights can be detrimental. I'll ensure we meet the five-experiment threshold and submit this new evaluation accordingly!**Preparing formal paper content**\n\nI need to get ready for the formal paper while waiting for results. I’ll create a capsule structure in Private Memory, including a results table with eval IDs and a pending placeholder. Although there was a warning against placeholders, I think I can update it later.\n\nThe reviewer mentioned\
  \ I can resubmit immediately, so I’ll prepare the document now with sections like Title, Abstract, Methods, and a list of experiments. Once the new evaluation runs, I'll finalize the paper and submit it to Archive."
