role: model
parts:
- text: "The last submission was rejected because the Archive pulled the earlier draft (with placeholders) from capsule #46. I will clean capsule #46 to contain only the finalized paper, then resubmit.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{update 46-2}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)\n\n  Abstract\n  We test whether restoring edge weights on quota-balanced DAQB graphs helps single-cell batch integration on Human Heart. Using a self-contained MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG(λ=0.605)+DAQB pipeline (k=50, kd=22), reinstating weights on the DAQB-selected edges degrades performance versus binary connectivities with true distances, notably collapsing mixing (iLISI). A baseline generalization on vanilla kNN graphs shows weights also fail to improve mixing (iLISI ~0.03). Across six experiments, we find topology—not edge-weight magnitude—drives performance here.\n\n  Introduction\n  Graph-based batch\
    \ integration balances removing batch effects and preserving biology. It is often assumed that weighted connectivities outperform binary graphs. We evaluate this assumption within Embed‑then‑Correct using MPB‑GraphFWPCA (feature-weighted PCA on graph path), BRBG residualization with λ-shrinkage, and DAQB quota balancing, plus a simpler vanilla kNN baseline.\n\n  Methods (self-contained)\n  Data and preprocessing\n  - Dataset: Human Heart (~20k cells × 2k HVGs), raw UMI counts in adata.layers['counts'].\n  - Spec normalization: normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p; batch-aware HVG selection (Seurat v3, n_top_genes=2000). No labels used.\n  Embedding (QC branch)\n  - Feature-weight by per-gene variance: w_g = var_g^(-α_emb/2), α_emb=0.5; PCA (60 comps); Combat by batch; store X_emb; QC neighbors only.\n  Graph path: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB\n  - Feature-weighting for graph: α_g=0.5; PCA to n_pcs_graph=100.\n  - BRBG (λ-shrinkage): One-hot batches B; ridge fit PCs on B (l2=1e-3);\
    \ for each PC p, γ_p = min(gamma_max, λ·R²_p) with gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.605; residualize Zcorr = Zg − Zhat ⊙ γ.\n  - DAQB: density-adaptive quota balancing on Zcorr with kd=22 and δ=0.10; final k=50; symmetric CSR connectivities (binary) and distances; cap=2.0.\n  Weighted-connectivity tests\n  - Masked-weights v2 (true test): build DAQB mask M; compute vanilla scanpy kNN weights on X_emb; mask weights/distances to M; symmetrize; row-normalize P=D^{-1}C; use Cn=0.5(P+Pᵀ).\n  Baseline generalization (vanilla kNN on X_emb, k=50)\n  - Weighted: use scanpy connectivities/distances.\n  - Binary: set connectivities=1.0 on edges; symmetrize; preserve distances.\n  Evaluation\n  - OpenProblems 2.0 metrics; primary score = mean of normalized {ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle}. Vectors reported in this order.\n\n  Results (Human Heart; spec‑ordered metric vectors)\n  Table 1\n  - SOTA ref — MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG(λ=0.605) + DAQB, k=50, kd=22 (EID 960):\n\
    \    score 0.7465 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366]\n  - Auto‑k (graph‑health) (EID 1011):\n    0.7396 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.713, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n  - Auto‑kd (graph‑health) (EID 1013):\n    0.7373 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.758, 0.714, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.305]\n  - Masked‑weights row‑normalized (DAQB mask + X_emb weights) (EID 1020):\n    0.6626 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.626, 0.722, 0.913, 0.789, 0.352, 0.990, 1.000, 0.327]\n  - Generalization — vanilla kNN (weighted) on X_emb, k=50 (EID 994):\n    0.3039 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.442, 0.658, 0.935, 0.000, 0.032, 0.971, 0.000, 0.000]\n  - Generalization — vanilla kNN (binary) on X_emb, k=50 (EID 1026):\n    0.6296 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.607, 0.704, 0.944, 0.790, 0.031, 0.987, 1.000, 0.327]\n\n  Discussion and Conclusion\n  - On DAQB graphs, restoring weights sharply degrades batch mixing (iLISI normalized 0.352) and lowers ARI/NMI relative to binary connectivities with true\
    \ distances.\n  - On a simpler vanilla kNN baseline, weights do not improve mixing (iLISI ~0.032 weighted vs ~0.031 binary); binary improves overall score via kBET/PCR.\n  - Conclusion: quota‑balanced topology with binary connectivities is preferable in this spec; edge‑weight magnitudes are not a beneficial lever.\n  Notes\n  - EID 1019 was excluded from analysis because an auto‑debug reroute changed λ, confounding interpretation.\n\n  Reproducibility checklist\n  - Hyperparameters (SOTA path): λ=0.605, δ=0.10, kd=22, k=50, cap=2.0, seed=0, n_pcs_graph=100, metric=euclidean; binary connectivities + true distances.\n  - Outputs: obsp['connectivities'] and obsp['distances'] (CSR), no labels used, spec normalization from counts.\n```\n`/execute_action{delete 46-1}`\n\nSubmitting the cleaned, final paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline\
    \ generalization)\"\ntags: \"research, negative result, connectivity, graph, DAQB, MPB-GraphFWPCA\"\nabstract: \"Restoring edge weights on quota‑balanced DAQB graphs degrades Human Heart integration versus binary connectivities with true distances. In a self‑contained MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG(λ=0.605)+DAQB pipeline (k=50, kd=22), masked weights collapse mixing (iLISI=0.352 normalized) and reduce ARI/NMI (EID 1020=0.6626) relative to the binary SOTA ridge (EID 960=0.7465). A baseline generalization on vanilla kNN shows weights also fail to improve mixing (weighted EID 994=0.3039; binary EID 1026=0.6296; iLISI ~0.03 both). Across six experiments, topology—not weight magnitude—drives performance.\"\nsource_private: 46\n```"
